# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored (`.env*` with an `!.env.example`
# exception) — do not commit real credentials.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL — interim content database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# See documentation/technical-specification/BLOCKERS.md §5. This database holds
# site CONTENT (tours, destinations, group departures, brochure metadata) plus
# the `AdminUser` table behind /admin. It holds NO LEADS — those are log-only
# (BLOCKERS.md §6) and that prohibition is NOT overridden.
#
# The "and has NO admin UI" clause that used to be on this line was removed on
# 2026-09-17: the user permanently reversed the zero-admin rule and the CMS is
# now built into this app. See memory/project_gty_admin_reversal.md.
#
# LEAVING THIS UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE, not a broken one. The composition root
# (src/content/content-adapter.ts) falls back to FixtureContentAdapter when
# DATABASE_URL is absent, so the site builds and runs with no database at all —
# which is how the test suite, `npm run build` and the Playwright suite run today.
#
# Local development with the docker-compose `db` service:
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://gty:gty_local_dev_password@localhost:5432/gty_content?schema=public"
#
# Deployed (Railway): the same Postgres INSTANCE as the CRM, but its own database
# /schema and its own credentials. It is NOT the CRM's database — never point this
# at the CRM's tables.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>:<port>/<database>?schema=gty_site_content"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRM_LEADS_ENDPOINT / CRM_API_KEY — lead submission (NOT YET USABLE)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reserved for `src/server/crm-client.ts`. Both are UNSET on purpose and reading
# them does nothing today: the CRM's lead-creation endpoint, payload schema and
# auth mechanism are still unconfirmed (BLOCKERS.md §1a, CLAUDE.md §10/§12), and
# `submitLeadToCrm` deliberately makes no network call rather than guessing a
# contract. Filling these in has no effect until that stub is replaced.
#
# UNSET IS THE SUPPORTED MODE, exactly as with DATABASE_URL above. With no CRM
# configured, `POST /api/leads` still validates every submission, still returns
# 200 to the visitor, and records the lead in the server log — which, since this
# site persists NO lead data of its own (BLOCKERS.md §6), is the only copy that
# exists. Grep `LEAD-SUBMISSION` to extract them.
#
# When the CRM ships the endpoint, these become a plain `fetch()` with
# `Authorization: Bearer ${CRM_API_KEY}` — see the worked example in the TODO in
# src/server/crm-client.ts.
# CRM_LEADS_ENDPOINT="https://<crm-host>/api/public/leads"
# CRM_API_KEY="<website-lead-submission-api-key>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTH_SECRET — admin session signing key (REQUIRED for /admin)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs and encrypts the JWT session cookie issued by NextAuth v5. Anyone holding
# this value can mint a valid admin session for this deployment, so treat it as a
# credential: never commit it, never reuse the development value in production.
#
# Generate one:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# UNSET IS NOT A SUPPORTED MODE IN PRODUCTION, unlike the variables above.
# src/server/auth/auth-base-config.ts THROWS at boot when NODE_ENV=production and
# this is missing, rather than quietly serving an admin area whose sessions can be
# forged. In development and in the test suite it falls back to a fixed,
# checked-in string so a hot reload does not sign you out mid-edit.
# AUTH_SECRET="<output of: openssl rand -base64 32>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUDINARY_* — media storage for the admin CMS (Phase C)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backs the cover image, gallery and brochure PDF uploads on /admin/tours/*.
# All three are read ONLY by src/server/uploads/cloudinary.ts, which runs
# server-side. `CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET` signs the upload request and MUST NEVER be
# exposed to the browser — there is no `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variant of any of these on
# purpose, and the browser never talks to Cloudinary directly. It POSTs the file
# to `/api/admin/uploads`, which is behind the admin session.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE, like DATABASE_URL and unlike AUTH_SECRET. With no
# Cloudinary configured, `isCloudinaryConfigured()` is false, the upload route
# answers 503 with that reason, and the admin form's upload controls disable
# themselves and SAY SO rather than failing at the moment a file is chosen. The
# rest of the tour editor is unaffected — but note a tour then cannot be
# PUBLISHED, because a cover image is required to publish (BLOCKERS.md item 11).
#
# Where to find these: https://console.cloudinary.com/ → Settings → API Keys.
# The cloud name is also shown on the dashboard as "Cloud name".
#
# Uploads go to the `gty/tours/...` folder with a Cloudinary-assigned unique
# public_id. Nothing is ever uploaded with `overwrite`, so replacing an image
# never destroys the asset the live site is still serving.
# CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME="<cloud name from the Cloudinary dashboard>"
# CLOUDINARY_API_KEY="<api key>"
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET="<api secret — a credential, never commit a real one>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL / ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD — first admin user (one-time use)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read ONLY by `npm run db:seed:admin` (prisma/seed-admin.ts), which upserts a
# single AdminUser row with a bcrypt-hashed password. There is no signup flow and
# no password-reset flow — this script is the only way an admin account comes into
# existence, and re-running it with an existing email ROTATES that user's password.
#
# These are intentionally NOT set here and should generally NOT be written into
# .env at all. Prefer passing them inline for the one command that needs them, so
# the password never rests in a file:
#
#   bash/zsh:
#     ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL="owner@example.com" ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD='<passphrase>' \
#       npm run db:seed:admin
#
#   PowerShell:
#     $env:ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL="owner@example.com"
#     $env:ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD='<passphrase>'
#     npm run db:seed:admin
#     Remove-Item Env:ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD
#
# Minimum length is 12 characters (src/server/auth/password.ts). A passphrase of
# several unrelated words beats a short scramble — it is both easier to type and
# harder to crack. No composition rules are enforced, deliberately.
# ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL="owner@example.com"
# ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD=""
